It wouldn't surprise me.  'Packs' of juice are generally restored from
concentrate, they've had processes done to them (in part to preserve the
juice) that mess with the materials in them.  I very much expect that
the natural yeasts that were present will have been killed.  It would be
quite bad for juice boxes to have active fermentation going on; not only
would they taste off, not only would there be concerns about kids
getting drunk at school, but having the juice boxes blow up in transit
because of the carbon dioxide build up would get annoying.

So, the concentration and restoration processes kill most of the natural
yeasts and whatnot, producing a relatively sterile environment full of
sugar.  Leave it open and of course you're going to get an undesired
result (if you're looking for good fermentation) -- it's a haven for
just about anything that likes sugar, water, and lack of competition (at
least initially).

OTOH, during Prohibition days in the US, the california grape industry
would put notices on their grape juice concentrate saying something much
like "do not put yeast in this because it would make wine and that would
be illegal".  I expect it wouldn't have made *good* wine, but it would
ferment and make something alcoholic, at least.

